      <data key="d10">[{"path": "primary_sources/m03/SFP-M03_v0.3.tex", "start": 257, "end": 279, "role": "statement", "name": "SFP-M03_v0.3.tex", "excerpt": "257: \\section{The workspace theorem}\n258: \\label{sec:main}\n259: \\begin{theorem}[Sharp worst-case observation overhead]\n260: \\label{thm:main}\n261: Under Section~\\ref{sec:contract}, let $\\mathcal F$ be a full observation\n262: fiber of initial width $K$, and let a common preparation-compatible\n263: task have finite known-factor cost $h$. Then\n264: \\begin{equation}\n265:  h\\le\\HH(\\mathcal F;\\calT,\\eps)\\le\\max\\{h,K+1\\}.\n266:  \\label{eq:main}\n267: \\end{equation}\n268: The same interface $\\Ust$ is used for both costs. The upper bound is\n269: attained by finite probing and preparation to a known realization.\n270: If one protected row has strict initial slack, or a specified initial\n271: slot is known to be idle, then $\\HH=h$. Also $\\HH=h$ whenever\n272: $h\\ge K+1$.\n273: \n274: For every graph in the stated class there is a saturated positive\n275: response at $K=N$ whose full observation fiber and calibration terminal\n276: $F=P$ satisfy $h=N$ and $\\HH=N+1$. On the Fano graph another saturated\n… Полный диапазон см. в источнике."}]</data>
