      <data key="d10">[{"path": "primary_sources/m04/SFP-M04_v0.2.tex", "start": 140, "end": 156, "role": "statement", "name": "SFP-M04_v0.2.tex", "excerpt": "140: \\section{Matched path resources}\\label{sec:resources}\n141: Fix a common $c&gt;0$, a protected set of responses, and regular endpoints $S_0,S_1$ of initial available width $K$. For a trial peak width $H\\ge K$, pad each endpoint by output-attached zero-input vertices without internal edges, matching the idle factor slots. All $H$ slots are counted. Define $H_{\\rm phys}(S_0,S_1)$ as the least such $H$ admitting a protected regular physical path with this fixed $c$; define $H_{\\rm fact}(\\Pi S_0,\\Pi S_1)$ by continuous row-stochastic factor paths with the identical response protection and padding convention. If no path exists the value is $+\\infty$.\n142: \\begin{corollary}\\label{cor:peaks}\n143: Under this matched contract,\n144: \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:peaks}\n145:  H_{\\rm phys}(S_0,S_1)=H_{\\rm fact}(\\Pi S_0,\\Pi S_1).\n146: \\end{equation}\n147: The same conclusion holds for common terminal predicates pulled back through $\\Pi$, with preparation allowed.\n148: \\end{corollary}\n149: \\begin{proof}\n150: The continuous projection in Lemma~\\ref{lem:projection} takes any admissible physical $H$-path to an admissible factor $H$-path. Conversely, normalize the padded $S_0$, lift a factor $H$-path using \\eqref{eq:section} with one $G&gt;\\sum_i c_i$, and reverse the normalization of the padded $S_1$. The preparation stages fix the protected response. These three paths concatenate without additional vertices. Thus the feasible sets of integer peaks coincide. For a pullback terminal choose its factor endpoint and its section realization; the same argument applies.\n151: \\end{proof}\n152: An unused regular vertex may change $\\Lambda_{RR}$ while leaving $C$ unchanged. This is admissible here because full DtN is not protected. A terminal prescribing $B_{\\eff,k}$ is also not automatically a boundary-only test. Full-response targets, fixed individual edges, power constraints, and new measuring ports require separate matching arguments.\n153: \n154: \\subsection{Normalized protection and scale changes}\n155: If only $F$ is protected, endpoints may have different positive $c$. At fixed $A,B$, use star scales $q_k&gt;x_k(c)=\\sum_i c_iA_{ik}$ and set\n156: \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:scale}"}]</data>
