      <data key="d10">[{"path": "primary_sources/m05/sections/parity_theorem.tex", "start": 4, "end": 46, "role": "statement", "name": "parity_theorem.tex", "excerpt": "4: The orientation comparison also gives a null prediction for a larger\n5: class than Euclidean gradient learning. Consider a driftless,\n6: residual-linear law\n7: \\begin{equation}\n8:  \\dot x=A(x)e,\\qquad e=q-C(x),\\qquad\n9:  P(x)=J(x)A(x),\\qquad L(x)=A(x)P(x)^{-1},\n10:  \\label{eq:general-residual-linear}\n11: \\end{equation}\n12: where $A$ has $m$ columns, $C$ is $C^4$, $A$ is $C^3$, and $P$ is\n13: invertible on the regular neighborhood of interest. Assume that targets\n14: $q=C+P^{-1}w$ realizing the required positive and negative output\n15: velocities are admissible. Then $JL=I$. The rule need not be a gradient\n16: in the stated coordinates. No state-independent drift, time-dependent\n17: rule, or unmodeled optimizer variable is added to\n18: \\eqref{eq:general-residual-linear}.\n19: \n20: For a common smooth subsequent response $F_T$, define\n21: \\begin{align}\n22:  I_{\\rm odd}(s,T)=\\Odd(s,T)&amp;=\\frac{F_T(H_+(s,x))-F_T(H_-(s,x))}{2},\\\\\n23:  I_{\\rm even}(s,T)&amp;=\\frac{F_T(H_+(s,x))+F_T(H_-(s,x))}{2}-F_T(x).\n… Полный диапазон см. в источнике."}]</data>
