      <data key="d10">[{"path": "primary_sources/m06/sections/02_model.tex", "start": 66, "end": 80, "role": "statement", "name": "02_model.tex", "excerpt": "66: Every source starts with total conductance $\\sum_{i,e}g_{ie}^0=1$, and its response is $C_{G_N}(g)=\\sum_i S(g_i)$. Preparation varies over the entire compact family $\\Delta^N$. Each star begins with conductance mass $1/N$; both its mass and its normalized shape subsequently evolve under \\cref{eq:learning-law}.\n67: \n68: \\subsection{A static equivalent with a different learning rate}\\label{subsec:static-example}\n69: Set every source shape to $(1/3,1/3,1/3)$, so every source edge initially has conductance $1/(3N)$. Its $N$ stars give $C_0=(1/9,1/9,1/9)$. The triangle with all three conductances $1/9$ has exactly the same response and uses total conductance $1/3$, within the allowed unit budget. At any symmetric star,\n70: \\[\n71:  DS=\\frac19\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;2&amp;-1\\\\2&amp;-1&amp;2\\\\-1&amp;2&amp;2\\end{pmatrix},\n72:  \\qquad DS\\,DS^\\top=\\frac19 I_3.\n73: \\]\n74: Summing the $N$ contributions to \\cref{eq:response-dynamics} gives\n75: \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:star-triangle-example}\n76:  K_{G_N}(g^0)=N\\frac{1}{3N}DS\\,DS^\\top=\\frac1{27}I_3,\n77:  \\qquad K_{\\triangle}(z^0)=\\frac19 I_3.\n78: \\end{equation}\n79: For any target $q\\ne C_0$, the triangle's initial response velocity is three times that of the star ensemble, as summarized in \\cref{fig:static-learning}. This example isolates the failure of static reduction. The identical source stars can themselves be merged into one star of mass one, which preserves all subsequent responses by homogeneity. The lower bound growing with $N$ instead concerns the full family of differing initial shapes.\n80: \\input{figures/01_static_learning}"}]</data>
