      <data key="d10">[{"path": "WEAK_TASK_LEARNING_20261029/JPA_ADAPTATION_20261105/source/sections/02_model_results.tex", "start": 77, "end": 105, "role": "statement", "name": "02_model_results.tex", "excerpt": "77: As a special case, choose a sufficiently small $\\eta&gt;0$ and prepare $g_1$ with $K_1=K_0+\\eta I_2$.\n78: For a unit vector $v\\in\\R^2$, train separately from $g_0$ and $g_1$ toward the same fixed target $y=\\phi_0+\\varepsilon v$ using $\\dot g=-J^\\top(\\phi-y)$.\n79: Writing $E_k^\\varepsilon(t)=\\frac12\\|\\phi(g_k^\\varepsilon(t))-y\\|^2$, for each fixed $H&lt;\\infty$,\n80: \\begin{equation}\\label{eq:future}\n81:  \\frac{E_1^\\varepsilon(t)}{E_0^\\varepsilon(t)}\n82:  \\longrightarrow e^{-2\\eta t}\\quad(\\varepsilon\\to0)\n83: \\end{equation}\n84: uniformly for $0\\le t\\le H$ and $\\|v\\|=1$.\n85: For every $0&lt;\\tau\\le H$ a single sufficiently small nonzero amplitude therefore gives strictly smaller prepared loss throughout $[\\tau,H]$ for all unit directions.\n86: \\end{theorem}\n87: \n88: Unlike full matrix programming, a positive scalar gain multiplies $K$ without changing its condition number, eigenvectors, or normalized cross-channel response.\n89: Common conductance rescaling gives $\\phi(cg)=\\phi(g)$ and $K(cg)=c^{-2}K(g)$, but also gives $Q(cg)=c^2Q(g)$, so only the trivial rescaling preserves the norm.\n90: \n91: The three entries of $K$ instead allow changes in learning geometry.\n92: For distinct eigenvalues, increasing the slow rate at a fixed fastest rate improves conditioning; a small isospectral change rotates the response directions.\n93: Reducing the magnitude of a nonzero off-diagonal entry at fixed diagonals weakens normalized cross-channel response.\n94: These local endpoint shifts and their weak-task interpretation are given in \\cref{sec:nonscalar}.\n95: \n96: Preparation precedes the choice of $v$; the amplitude threshold may depend on $H,\\tau,\\eta$ and both states.\n… Полный диапазон см. в источнике."}]</data>
